From 6ded35013dd5952330f126bdd76a200bc653406a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bill Hlavacek <bill.hlavacek@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 10:03:49 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] bngsim: test product molecularity as BioNetGen does, and list
 each product molecule once

A rule whose products are written apart (A.B -> A + B) may fire only where
its products end up in different complexes. NFsim made the test only for
one-reactant rules, only under complex bookkeeping (-cb/-bscb), and only
between the two ends of each bond the rule deletes, all of which had to
separate:

- Without -cb, L(r!1).R(l!1) -> L(r) + R(l) opened every ring: 100
  four-member rings L.R.L.R all broke, where BNG2.pl's network keeps 100.
- A bimolecular rule was never tested. L(r!1,c).R(l!1) + C(x) ->
  L(r,c!2).C(x!2) + R(l) on those rings bound every C; BNG2.pl generates no
  reaction for it.
- A(b!1,c!2).B(a!1).C(a!2) -> A(b,c) + B(a) + C(a) on rings where B and C
  are also bonded broke every ring: the ends of each deleted bond separate,
  but B and C do not.
- A bond deleted inside one product pattern was tested as if it had to
  separate: A(x!1,y!2,z!3).B(x!1,y!2).C(a!3) -> A(x,y!2,z).B(x,y!2) + C(a)
  never fired. BNG2.pl fires it.
- A molecule the rule deletes takes its bonds with it (BNG2.pl writes no
  DeleteBond for them), so A(b!1,x!2).B(a!1).X(a!2) -> A(b,x) + B(a)
  DeleteMolecules on rings A.B.X separates A from B; the test walked
  through X and never fired it.

The loader records which product pattern each reactant molecule maps to
(from the rule's Map). finalize() records the molecules the rule acts on
(one per template its transformations map) with their product patterns,
and the molecules it deletes. productsSeparate then requires every pair of
acted-on molecules in different product patterns to be disconnected once
the rule's deleted bonds and molecules are gone, for any number of reactants and whatever complex bookkeeping says.
Bonds the rule adds, and bonds to molecules it creates, need no edge: their
ends are acted on and share one product pattern, so any connection between
two patterns runs between two tested molecules over bonds already there.

The search runs from both ends in turn, each allowed four times the
molecules of the last, so when the ends are apart it costs the smaller
side, and it reuses its buffers: a refused match in a gel had spent half
its time allocating.

Without -cb a bimolecular rule may also match two molecules of one complex.
getListOfProducts appended each reactant's bonded neighbourhood to the
product list, and two such neighbourhoods overlap when the second reactant
lies beyond the first's traversal limit. A molecule listed twice was added
to the Molecules observables twice (removal is idempotent, addition is
not), so a conserved count of 30 read 84-98. Each molecule is now listed
once.

Not tested yet: a product held apart only by a molecule the rule deletes,
or tied to the rest of its pattern only by "." (neither has a molecule the
rule acts on), and the "." half (ensureConnected), which NFsim ignores.
